gotten a really bad grade

00:00:13.665 --> 00:00:15.398
on something in med school.

00:00:16.885 --> 00:00:19.105
- Who hasn't? I mean, med school is tough.

00:00:19.105 --> 00:00:22.105
Like some of the subjects
just absolutely beat you down.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:24.655
I dunno why biochemistry
really stopped me the most.

00:00:24.655 --> 00:00:27.075
- Never have I ever pulled an all-nighter.

00:00:27.075 --> 00:00:30.405
- Come on, who hasn't pulled
an all-nighter in med school.

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:32.625
If any of them say they
have not, I don't buy it.

00:00:32.625 --> 00:00:34.865
- I don't believe in all-nighter.

00:00:34.865 --> 00:00:37.075
- She's never pulled an all-nighter?

00:00:37.075 --> 00:00:38.465
That's really impressive.

00:00:38.465 --> 00:00:41.105
- I would go to bed at
like 10:30 every night.

00:00:41.105 --> 00:00:42.805
- Look she knows herself
,that's impressive,

00:00:42.805 --> 00:00:43.638
that's a good thing.

00:00:43.638 --> 00:00:46.115
The fact that she's organized
enough to prevent that

00:00:46.115 --> 00:00:47.745
from being a problem, amazing.

00:00:47.745 --> 00:00:49.685
- Never have I ever
messed up when learning

00:00:49.685 --> 00:00:50.993
or trying to do something.

00:00:50.993 --> 00:00:51.826
- Oh God.

00:00:51.826 --> 00:00:52.659
- And that's what learning is about.

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:54.015
- Even if you read about doing something,

00:00:54.015 --> 00:00:56.125
in practice, it's a
completely different story.

00:00:56.125 --> 00:00:58.215
Like you learn on a mannequin
and you go to see a patient

00:00:58.215 --> 00:01:00.215
and then you realize they
are human not a mannequin

00:01:00.215 --> 00:01:01.665
and they have their own intricacies

00:01:01.665 --> 00:01:03.745
and anatomic anomalies, it gets hard.

00:01:03.745 --> 00:01:05.405
- For us we are practicing intubation,

00:01:05.405 --> 00:01:08.735
which is basically a putting
tube in someone's mouth.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:10.459
So that they can breathe if
they can't breathe on their own.

00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:14.025
And it was mannequins obviously
but the very first thing

00:01:14.025 --> 00:01:16.475
the doctor said was, "Don't go like this,

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:17.805
or else you're gonna break their teeth."

00:01:17.805 --> 00:01:20.575
- And the actual mannequins
have a design in them

00:01:20.575 --> 00:01:21.785
so if you are pulling like that

00:01:21.785 --> 00:01:24.255
it makes like a huge
crunch noise to scare you

00:01:24.255 --> 00:01:25.378
and teach you that you could
could be hurting their teeth.

00:01:25.378 --> 00:01:28.445
- Never have I ever
fallen asleep in class.

00:01:28.445 --> 00:01:29.525
- I remember.

00:01:29.525 --> 00:01:30.975
This is such a funny story to tell.

00:01:30.975 --> 00:01:33.065
When I was in undergrad,

00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:34.855
the professor had a very strict rule,

00:01:34.855 --> 00:01:37.905
if you were going to fall
asleep in the classroom

00:01:37.905 --> 00:01:39.855
you had to sit in the back row

00:01:39.855 --> 00:01:41.615
and rest your head against the wall

00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:43.115
so it looks like you're paying attention.

00:01:43.115 --> 00:01:45.745
That was a legit rule and
I thought like he was being

00:01:45.745 --> 00:01:47.685
maybe sarcastic or something so one night

00:01:47.685 --> 00:01:48.775
where I didn't get good sleep,

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:51.385
I'm in the second to last row
and I fell asleep on my arm.

00:01:51.385 --> 00:01:53.045
He kicked me out of the class.

00:01:53.045 --> 00:01:54.775
And at the end of that
class there was a quiz

00:01:54.775 --> 00:01:56.225
that I then got a zero on.

00:01:56.225 --> 00:01:57.665
I really thought he was kidding.

00:01:57.665 --> 00:01:58.678
- I am not a doctor.

00:01:59.655 --> 00:02:01.965
- By the way doctors aren't
also good at pronouncing

00:02:01.965 --> 00:02:02.798
these medical terms.

00:02:02.798 --> 00:02:05.245
And Sam already has told me
that I have to pronounce these

00:02:05.245 --> 00:02:07.335
so be prepared for some bad pronunciation.

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:08.724
English is my second language.

00:02:08.724 --> 00:02:11.905
Borborygmi, borborygmi

00:02:14.015 --> 00:02:16.065
This is actually just like
the sound your stomach makes.

00:02:16.065 --> 00:02:17.388
So like this isn't that exciting.

00:02:17.388 --> 00:02:18.755
- Borborygmi.

00:02:18.755 --> 00:02:20.195
- Borborygmi.

00:02:20.195 --> 00:02:21.075
- Borborygmi.

00:02:21.075 --> 00:02:22.095
- Borborygmi.

00:02:22.095 --> 00:02:22.928
- Borborygmi.

00:02:22.928 --> 00:02:25.095
(ringing)

00:02:25.095 --> 00:02:26.285
- I feel like it has
something to do with the butt.

00:02:26.285 --> 00:02:27.885
- It's an ADHD problem.

00:02:27.885 --> 00:02:29.718
- Being born with an extra of something.

00:02:29.718 --> 00:02:31.025
- Like you got a bad butt.

00:02:31.025 --> 00:02:32.515
- Why is everyone talking about butts?

00:02:32.515 --> 00:02:34.215
Choledo, ooh this is good.

00:02:34.215 --> 00:02:38.275
Choledocholithiasis.

00:02:38.275 --> 00:02:41.825
I've actually pronounced
this term in residency

00:02:41.825 --> 00:02:43.125
'cause I couldn't pronounce it.

00:02:43.125 --> 00:02:44.785
Choledocholithiasis.

00:02:44.785 --> 00:02:46.495
I don't even need to look at it.

00:02:46.495 --> 00:02:48.065
'Cause it was so hard for me to pronounce.

00:02:48.065 --> 00:02:49.765
I actually practiced, reciting this.

00:02:49.765 --> 00:02:51.577
'Cause this means you have a gallstone

00:02:51.577 --> 00:02:54.225
in the common bile duct.

00:02:54.225 --> 00:02:55.170
- Choledocholithiasis.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.485
(laughs)

00:02:56.485 --> 00:02:57.465
- That guy is my favorite.

00:02:57.465 --> 00:02:58.975
Ankylosing spondylitis.

00:02:58.975 --> 00:03:00.027
This is an easy one.

00:03:00.027 --> 00:03:01.868
- Ankylosing spondylitis

00:03:01.868 --> 00:03:04.345
- Ankylosing spondylitis

00:03:05.285 --> 00:03:06.955
- I like when they call it, Ankylosing

00:03:06.955 --> 00:03:09.663
Temporoparietalis. But like,

00:03:09.663 --> 00:03:11.835
I think this is just like a headache.

00:03:11.835 --> 00:03:12.785
You have to take it Latin.

00:03:12.785 --> 00:03:16.415
So temporary means your
temples, parietal is the sides.

00:03:16.415 --> 00:03:18.428
Parietalis, I don't know what this is.

00:03:18.428 --> 00:03:20.335
- Temporoparietalis.

00:03:20.335 --> 00:03:22.335
Oh, it is a scalp muscles.

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:25.998
Sphygmomanometer, remember
Dilshad couldn't get this.

00:03:25.998 --> 00:03:27.798
- Sphygmomanometer

00:03:27.798 --> 00:03:29.715
Sphygmomanometer.

00:03:29.715 --> 00:03:31.075
- It's Sphygmomanometer.

00:03:31.075 --> 00:03:31.955
Is that how you say it?

00:03:31.955 --> 00:03:34.410
I thought it was Sphygmomanometer.

00:03:37.843 --> 00:03:40.243
- What's the craziest
thing you've seen in the ER

00:03:40.243 --> 00:03:42.776
a guy with the thing used
for guacamole, I guess

00:03:42.776 --> 00:03:45.703
to like grind it I don't know
what that thing's called.

00:03:45.703 --> 00:03:47.023
He put that up his rectum.

00:03:47.023 --> 00:03:48.493
- I feel like we did
like a whole Reddit thing

00:03:48.493 --> 00:03:50.573
of people putting weird
things into their bodies.

00:03:50.573 --> 00:03:52.433
While with Dr. Jones on the
channel said something similar.

00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:55.483
- The things that come along
with gynecology are endless.

00:03:55.483 --> 00:03:58.133
Anything you can imagine
has probably happened.

00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:00.083
- What's the worst smell
you've experienced.

00:04:00.083 --> 00:04:01.152
- I don't know if you want to hear this.

00:04:01.152 --> 00:04:04.313
- An infected gangrenous
wound by far the worst.

00:04:04.313 --> 00:04:06.383
- Has an incident ever freaked you out.

00:04:06.383 --> 00:04:07.216
There was a guy that came

00:04:07.216 --> 00:04:09.123
in one time and I unwrapped his foot

00:04:09.123 --> 00:04:10.743
and there was magnets all over his foot.

00:04:10.743 --> 00:04:11.923
And it was my job.

00:04:11.923 --> 00:04:13.948
Lucky me to go and clean up the maggots

00:04:13.948 --> 00:04:16.943
and the maggots pretty much
ate like down to the bone.

00:04:16.943 --> 00:04:19.193
- Well the maggots
usually eat dead tissue.

00:04:19.193 --> 00:04:20.583
They're not feeding on live tissue.

00:04:20.583 --> 00:04:21.573
So that means there's some kind

00:04:21.573 --> 00:04:23.113
of gangrene already setting in there.

00:04:23.113 --> 00:04:25.693
- How often do you have
to look up a diagnosis?

00:04:25.693 --> 00:04:26.573
- Every day.

00:04:26.573 --> 00:04:28.473
I mean, things are changing all the time.

00:04:28.473 --> 00:04:31.620
- You see a lot of the
same things all the time.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.603
And then like once in a
blue moon, you'll get a,

00:04:34.603 --> 00:04:36.843
you'll get something that
like you don't know at all.

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:37.803
- I mean, that's part of medicine.

00:04:37.803 --> 00:04:39.243
You just can't know everything.

00:04:39.243 --> 00:04:40.533
So yeah. You're going to look stuff up.

00:04:40.533 --> 00:04:41.366
When you look stuff up

00:04:41.366 --> 00:04:43.763
you become more comfortable
with dealing with the unknown.

00:04:43.763 --> 00:04:44.753
And that's what makes medicine fun.

00:04:44.753 --> 00:04:46.232
It's like an ever learning field.

00:04:46.232 --> 00:04:48.409
(upbeat music)

00:04:48.409 --> 00:04:52.243
- Do you still check web,
WebMD when you don't feel well?

00:04:52.243 --> 00:04:54.533
- Absolutely. First of all,
everyone still checks WebMD.

00:04:54.533 --> 00:04:55.653
Even when you become a doctor

00:04:55.653 --> 00:04:57.183
maybe you shift over to Medscape

00:04:57.183 --> 00:04:58.016
but little did you know WebMD

00:04:58.016 --> 00:05:00.453
and Medscape are the same company?

00:05:00.453 --> 00:05:02.513
- Do you rely on coffee to keep you going?

00:05:02.513 --> 00:05:04.733
- Now yes. Med school no.

00:05:04.733 --> 00:05:05.566
- Every single day.

00:05:05.566 --> 00:05:06.399
There's a schedule too.

00:05:06.399 --> 00:05:08.783
It's like for three months
coffee and then two week break.

00:05:08.783 --> 00:05:10.353
So that it works again.

00:05:10.353 --> 00:05:11.393
- That's a true doctor.

00:05:11.393 --> 00:05:13.893
He's like cycling his caffeine intake to

00:05:13.893 --> 00:05:16.233
make sure he's getting the
maximum benefit from it.

00:05:16.233 --> 00:05:18.353
- Have you ever successfully
diagnosed yourself

00:05:18.353 --> 00:05:19.186
with something?

00:05:19.186 --> 00:05:22.173
- That's classic med school
101, you learn a condition.

00:05:22.173 --> 00:05:24.753
You think you have the
condition, you learn a condition

00:05:24.753 --> 00:05:28.043
that occurs in one per
million people that comes

00:05:28.043 --> 00:05:28.876
with a headache.

00:05:28.876 --> 00:05:29.843
You have a headache.

00:05:29.843 --> 00:05:31.383
You say you have this condition.

00:05:31.383 --> 00:05:32.216
It's just normal.

00:05:32.216 --> 00:05:33.049
It's part of the process.

00:05:33.049 --> 00:05:35.443
- No, but I have successfully
misdiagnosed myself

00:05:35.443 --> 00:05:36.333
with a bunch of things.

00:05:36.333 --> 00:05:38.573
- What was your least favorite
part of medical school?

00:05:38.573 --> 00:05:39.406
Step one.

00:05:40.693 --> 00:05:42.542
- That's the first boards that you take

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:43.883
like the licensing exam.

00:05:43.883 --> 00:05:47.593
Oh, you have to know so much information.

00:05:47.593 --> 00:05:52.053
So much of it being pretty
practically speaking useless.

00:05:52.053 --> 00:05:52.886
Oh man.

00:05:52.886 --> 00:05:54.083
That was tough.

00:05:54.083 --> 00:05:55.613
Cause that decides a lot

00:05:55.613 --> 00:05:58.113
of times where you end up
in terms of your residency.

00:05:58.113 --> 00:05:58.953
So like.

00:05:58.953 --> 00:06:01.073
- Is it true that a lot
of med students experiment

00:06:01.073 --> 00:06:02.613
with drugs?

00:06:02.613 --> 00:06:03.723
caffeine all the time?

00:06:03.723 --> 00:06:04.663
- Caffeine yeah.

00:06:04.663 --> 00:06:06.996
- I wouldn't say experiment.

00:06:08.293 --> 00:06:10.696
- I feel like there's more to that story.

00:06:12.293 --> 00:06:15.133
Right from the ocean
dolphin and turtle safe.

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:16.500
I'm not joking either.

00:06:20.393 --> 00:06:22.383
Yay tea with honey.

00:06:22.383 --> 00:06:24.146
Menuca honey green tea combo.

00:06:25.383 --> 00:06:27.936
A little lemon with the
vitamin C kick. Yummy.

00:06:29.153 --> 00:06:29.986
No, no, no, no.

00:06:29.986 --> 00:06:32.343
get that apple cider vinegar out of here.

00:06:32.343 --> 00:06:34.241
Now it doesn't stop there.

00:06:34.241 --> 00:06:37.523
Stop that nonsense right now.

00:06:37.523 --> 00:06:39.613
The honey actually works
quite well for cough.

00:06:39.613 --> 00:06:41.703
The American Academy of
pediatricians actually

00:06:41.703 --> 00:06:43.443
recommends this as part
of their growing body

00:06:43.443 --> 00:06:45.593
of evidence that honey can work as well

00:06:45.593 --> 00:06:47.863
or better than some over the
counter cough suppressants.

00:06:47.863 --> 00:06:49.293
You just have to make
sure not to give honey

00:06:49.293 --> 00:06:51.336
to kids younger than the age of two.

00:06:55.883 --> 00:06:57.403
- This is just extra.

00:06:57.403 --> 00:06:59.483
You do not need to do this
for cough suppression.

00:06:59.483 --> 00:07:00.423
Just let it cough.

00:07:00.423 --> 00:07:01.643
Like if you have phlegm in your lungs

00:07:01.643 --> 00:07:03.773
and you're trying to get it
out, let your body get it out.

00:07:03.773 --> 00:07:04.823
This is a good thing.

00:07:07.533 --> 00:07:09.563
Drinking, lots of fluids
or a decent amount

00:07:09.563 --> 00:07:11.643
of fluids when you're sick
and phlegm is really good.

00:07:11.643 --> 00:07:12.953
Cause it actually thins the mucus

00:07:12.953 --> 00:07:14.896
and you're able to clear it easier.

00:07:16.403 --> 00:07:18.973
- Oh come on. This is like
a commercial for almonds.

00:07:18.973 --> 00:07:20.053
This is definitely sponsored.

00:07:20.053 --> 00:07:21.713
That's like saying, oh, your head hurts.

00:07:21.713 --> 00:07:24.068
Take a magnesium tablet come on.

00:07:24.068 --> 00:07:24.901
- Come on!

00:07:32.035 --> 00:07:34.533
- I mean, I just prefer
going out and shelling

00:07:34.533 --> 00:07:37.523
out the five bucks or so for
like the saline nasal spray.

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:38.356
That's really good.

00:07:38.356 --> 00:07:39.443
Cause it does clear out your sinuses.

00:07:39.443 --> 00:07:40.693
You can just like spit it back out.

00:07:40.693 --> 00:07:42.753
In fact, it's also really
good for allergies as well.

00:07:42.753 --> 00:07:43.983
So yeah, I'm for it.

00:07:43.983 --> 00:07:46.456
I just don't know like this
technique specifically.

00:07:48.373 --> 00:07:50.883
Never do this. You're going to
wake up with athlete's foot.

00:07:50.883 --> 00:07:52.713
Cause your foot has been soaked in water.

00:07:52.713 --> 00:07:53.983
It's all going to be all shriveled up.

00:07:53.983 --> 00:07:55.723
We don't live in the 18 hundreds anymore.

00:07:55.723 --> 00:07:57.776
Like you just don't
need to do this anymore.

00:08:02.413 --> 00:08:03.313
That's ridiculous.

00:08:08.463 --> 00:08:10.703
The reason you're having a
fever is to actually improve

00:08:10.703 --> 00:08:12.566
